// An immediate-mode user interface for sysl, sized for a panel on a microcontroller.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block and there is no `requires` block, and both absences are the
// point of the package. It depends on no other sysl package because it rasterizes nothing: the
// drawing is done by whatever the program hands it, through the `Painter` trait, so cairo on a
// desktop and PlutoVG on a board are the *caller's* choice and neither appears here.
//
// This is the block a consumer writes to reach it:
//
//     dependencies {
//       imui { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/imui", version = "0.1.2" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.1.2` on this repository.

package {
  name    = "imui"
  version = "0.1.2"

  // The oldest compiler this builds with.
  sysl = "0.8.0"
}

// **There is deliberately no `requires` block, and the module says so a second time by declaring
// `@no_alloc`** -- which the compiler then holds it to, file by file, including the tests.
//
// It asks the machine for nothing: no allocator, no operating system, no C library. Every piece of
// storage it uses is either a field of a struct the caller declared or a slice the caller handed
// over, which is the same bargain `sh.sysl.harness` makes and the reason either can be linked into
// an image for a board that has 520 KB of RAM and no heap worth the name.
//
// The one thing that is *not* claimed here: a backend will almost certainly need capabilities of
// its own. PlutoVG asks for `heap` and `posix`, and cairo asks for `heap` and `os`. That cost is
// the backend's and is visible in the backend's own manifest, which is exactly where a program
// should have to look for it.
